From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 21:09:20 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] drm/asahi: g16: Optionally run the completion work on
 the big cores

The poll work that retires completions, handles reports and publishes
the next commands is queued with WORK_CPU_UNBOUND on the per-CPU
system_highpri_wq, so it runs on the CPU that queued it: the one that
took the mailbox interrupt or fired the re-poll hrtimer, which the
work itself re-arms where it runs. On the J604 that is an efficiency
core at half the capacity of the performance cores, busy 46 % during
compute.light at depth 64 while the client runs on a performance core.

Add the runtime switch g16_worker_big (default 0, same as before). At
probe the driver takes the CPUs of maximum arch_scale_cpu_capacity()
and builds an unbound high-priority queue, aurora_g16_big, limited to
them. With 1 the poll work is queued there; with 2 it is queued on
system_highpri_wq on one such CPU chosen at probe (the last), with no
migration between passes. Either way the hrtimer callback and the
mailbox kick only queue the work, so a timer firing elsewhere does not
bring the pass back to the little core, and the timer re-armed by the
pass is started on the big CPU that runs it. Scheduler submit queues
(aurora_g16_hi, run-job and free-job) created while the switch is on
are limited to the same CPUs.

Nothing is queued while no GPU work is outstanding, so the switch costs
nothing at an idle desktop. The stats file gains a wbig: line: passes
on big and on other CPUs, passes per CPU, and the time from an
immediate queueing to the pass that serves it.

Assisted-by: LLM
